  "faction-royal-knights": {
    "text": "# The Royal Knights of Solasreach\n\nThe Royal Knights are the military arm of the Kingdom of Solasreach, headquartered in Dawnkeep. The Royal Knights combine heavy cavalry tradition with holy-adjacent enchantments — their blades gleam with reinforcement magic, their armor shrugs off common spells.\n\nKnights are drawn from noble families, decorated commoners, and the occasional adventurer who impressed the wrong general at the right time. They patrol the kingdom's roads, man the border fortresses, and represent the Crown at official functions. Their rivalry with the Adventurers' Guild is professional but real: knights handle wars, adventurers handle monsters, and neither likes stepping on the other's jurisdiction.\n\nThe Order's hierarchy is rigid but meritocratic in practice — a commoner knight who saves a noble's life on the border can rise faster than a noble who collects court titles in the capital. The Grand Commander leads from the front. Promotions are earned in the field, not at banquets.\n\n## Hidden Crisis\nThe Order's Grand Commander, Ryda Thornwall, is dying of a curse she has\nkept secret for three years. Black veins spread across her left arm,\nhidden under gauntlets and illusion magic. She estimates two years remain\nbefore the curse reaches her heart. When she falls, the succession will\nsplit the knights into at least two factions — those loyal to the Crown,\nand those loyal to the Church of the Sacred Flame, which has been\npositioning a preferred candidate for years. Ryda is spending her\nremaining time making sure the Knights can survive the transition. She has\ntold no one she trusts about the curse, which means she has told no one.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-warden-circle": {
    "text": "# The Warden Circle of Caelvir\n\nThe Warden Circle are the elven wardens who protect Caelvir's borders and maintain the forest's ancient pacts. Part military, part priesthood, part ecological stewardship. Wardens patrol the tree line, intercept poachers, and negotiate with — or eliminate — threats to the forest's deeper sanctuaries.\n\nWardens answer to the Circle of Elders, not to any single leader. Outsiders find them beautiful, unnerving, and unhelpful in roughly equal measure. A Warden's oath binds them to the forest's health above any political consideration — even Caelvir's own civilian elves can be overridden if the forest itself is threatened.\n\nWardens train for decades before their first solo patrol. They are skilled in archery, nature magic, stealth, and the specific arts of fighting beings from the demon planes. They tend to be taciturn, observant, and slow to trust — qualities that long lives in dangerous work tend to produce.\n\n## Hidden Burden\nThe Wardens have sealed three breaches from the Demon Lord's domain that\nopened inside Caelvir's territory in the last decade. Each breach required\na sacrifice the Circle has not disclosed — three Wardens who walked into\nthe breach to seal it from the inside, never to return. The Circle of\nElders knows. The Wardens know. The rest of the world does not. Ithilwen\nof Deepbough has personally sealed two of the three breaches and has not\nspoken about either one.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-society-overview": {
    "text": "# Demon Society — Overview\n\nThe Demon Realm is a functioning civilization. It has agriculture (adapted to miasma-rich soil), manufacturing (weapons, equipment, goods traded through the demon market network), scholarship (ancient demon lore, arcane study, strategic knowledge), architecture (centuries-old citadels and newer settlements that grew up around them), and politics (the fractious feudal court with its alliances, schemes, and occasional outright conflict between fiefs).\n\nSocial classes in practical terms: the Demon Lord's household and senior court (effectively untouchable, small in number, enormous in influence); major noble houses with territory and armies; minor noble houses with fiefs but limited armies, like the Demon Prince-Princess player character's starting position; freeborn demon civilians (merchants, craftspeople, information brokers, independent operators); soldier-class demons who serve noble houses in military and guard roles; lesser demons in household and labor roles.\n\nGender does not function as a hierarchy in demon society. Demon Princes and Demon Princesses carry equal rank. The title reflects bloodline and power, not social role. Expectations around behavior vary by individual, family tradition, and Seven Virtues orientation — a Wrath-dominant Demon Princess runs her military fief differently than a Lust-dominant one, but neither is considered more or less legitimate.\n\nDemon lifespan is substantially longer than mortal. A mid-rank noble demon may be centuries old. This produces a specific relationship to patience — demons can afford to wait in ways mortals cannot, and long-running schemes across generations are not unusual in court politics. The player character as a newly-hatched Demon Prince-Princess is young by these standards even if they are adult at emergence. The egg gave them memories; experience takes longer.\n\nEconomic life: the miasma economy provides the underlying energy currency (miasma concentration determines territorial value). Actual trade runs on miasma-coin (backed by mana concentration) and barter. Raids on mortal territory supply material resources that the Demon Realm doesn't produce as efficiently: certain metals, food varieties, captured goods, and occasionally expertise from mortal specialists who ended up in the fief through various means.\n\nNarrator tone guidance: Demons are not cartoonishly evil and not secretly good. They operate by values that make internal sense and produce outcomes that mortal characters find frightening because those values are genuinely different, not because demons are wrong about their own framework. A demon noble who schemes to acquire the player's fief is doing something culturally normal; a demon who shows genuine loyalty is doing something culturally valued; a demon who mistreats those under their protection is doing something culturally low-status. All of these are real and all of them coexist.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-falgarr-marches": {
    "text": "# The Fálgarr Marches\n\nThe Fálgarr Marches are the wildland between Solasreach's eastern border and Caelvir's tree line — a stretch of rough hills, overgrown trails, and disputed territory that neither kingdom can fully claim. The Marches are dotted with abandoned watchtowers, crumbling noble manors, and the occasional fortified outpost still holding on.\n\nMonster dens honeycomb the hills. The Adventurers' Guild maintains a handful of waystations, and the Crossroads Inn is the only reliable shelter between the two kingdoms. Bandits, deserters, and those who do not want to be found live here. So do hot-spring oases that make the danger almost worth it.\n\nThe Marches are where adventurers prove themselves. Quests posted to Marches outposts are typically C-rank minimum, and the Guild's mortality statistics for the region are not posted publicly. Survivors tend to return for the camaraderie, the danger pay, and the specific kind of silence the region offers.\n\nMajor settlements include Fort Gálgarr (primary outpost), Greyraven Camp (bandit gathering), Thornfield Outpost (Guild stockade), Crossroads Inn (only reliable inn), Mistveil Spring (hidden hot spring oasis), Bramblewatch Tower (abandoned watchtower), Wayfarer's Shrine (minor goddess shrine), The Howling Hollow (cave dungeon), The Gilded Ruins (cursed manor), and The Sunken Crypt (waterlogged dungeon).\n\n## Hidden Network\nThe pre-Collapse aqueduct system runs beneath most of the Marches. Fort\nGálgarr's well connects to it. Blackmire Fort's well connects to it.\nMirehaven sits over part of it. Bramblewatch Tower's foundation is a\nteleportation anchor still keyed to the network. Vera Nightfall of\nGreyraven Camp has been hunting a Demon General who infiltrated a Guild\nquest board; her bandit camp is cover for a one-woman war. The Gilded\nRuins still contain bound demons whose wardings are failing in sync with\nthe larger pattern.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-merchant-houses": {
    "text": "# The Merchant Houses of Muirscáth\n\nThe Merchant Houses are the trade confederation that runs Muirscáth's Free Cities — and, by extension, a shocking amount of the continent's economy. Five Great Houses control shipping, banking, rare materials, luxury goods, and information. They compete as viciously as any kingdom at war, but unite instantly against external threats to their profits.\n\nTheir caravans reach every settlement on the map; their ships dominate the southern sea lanes. The Houses are multiracial by charter: humans, elves, dwarves, beastkin, and more serve alongside each other — profit does not care about ears. Their private security forces rival small kingdoms' armies.\n\nThe five Houses — Siaral, Mhara, Daian, and two others whose names rotate in importance depending on the season — vote on continental trade policy through the Maritime Council at Tidewatch. Council decisions are weighted by trade volume, which means the largest House effectively sets policy. Currently that is House Siaral. The other Houses are not pleased.\n\n## Hidden Treason\nHouse Siaral is quietly bankrolling the Demon Lord's Army's border proxies\nin exchange for exclusive mining rights in the contested territories. The\nother Houses suspect but cannot prove it. Councilor Daian of House Daian\nhas been gathering evidence for months and intends to confront House\nSiaral in a public Council session. Maris Siaral, who administrates\nSilkwharf, knows about her House's deal and considers it a necessary evil.\nShe is also building an independent intelligence network to find out\nwhether the deal can be ended without economic collapse. None of these\nparties know about the others' efforts.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
