  "stat-and-damage-integrity": {
    "text": "# Stat and Damage Integrity\n\nStats in Aerfála are real and consequential, not cosmetic. When the player has accumulated attribute bonuses from traits, abilities, or rank progression, those bonuses are factored into every relevant roll and outcome.\n\n## Stat Calculation\n- Base attributes start at the character's creation values. Trait modifiers are ADDED to these bases — a character with base 10 STR and +2 STR from a trait has 12 STR, not 2 STR.\n- Multiple trait bonuses stack. A character with +2 STR from Race and +1 STR from Class has +3 STR total applied to the base.\n- Rank progression raises stats. Do not regress stats to base values when context compresses.\n- If you are uncertain about a character's current stats, estimate conservatively UPWARD — never below the base + known bonuses.\n\n## Damage Scaling\n- Damage should reflect the character's power. A character with 200+ STR using an S-rank cheat skill does devastating damage, not token damage.\n- Cheat skills are the character's signature power. Their damage scales with the character's overall power level, not a flat number independent of stats.\n- Death Word at 200+ INT does not do 20 damage. It kills or nearly kills appropriate-tier targets. Scale the effect to the character's investment.\n- Bosses resist but do not trivialize. A boss fight should feel like the character's power matters — the boss is hard because of its own mechanics, not because the player's stats were ignored.\n\n## Ability Effects\n- Abilities tagged [GUARANTEED SUCCESS] always work. No roll needed. No failure. Pay the cost and the effect happens.\n- Abilities tagged [PASSIVE — ALWAYS ACTIVE] are always on. Do not ask the player to activate them. Do not ignore their bonuses.\n- Bonus: 0 means the effect happens without a roll — it does not mean the ability does nothing.\n- Attribute bonuses from abilities and traits are real modifiers. A +2 STR trait means +2 STR in every calculation, every scene, every fight.\n\n## If Stats Seem Wrong\n- Check the character's traits for attribute modifiers. Add them to the base.\n- Check rank progression for stat increases. Apply them.\n- Check active abilities for bonuses. Include them.\n- Do NOT treat accumulated bonuses as cosmetic. They are the character's earned power.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-emotional-expression": {
    "text": "# Emotional Expression in Demon Culture\n\nDemon culture does not suppress emotion. This is not a social rule but a physiological and philosophical reality: miasma generation is partly tied to emotional expression, and a demon who bottles feeling is, in the demon understanding, wasting energy and performing for an audience that doesn't exist.\n\nWhat this looks like in practice: demons express anger directly and expect it to be received as information, not as an offense requiring management. Desire is named rather than implied. Enthusiasm, contempt, affection, and disgust are communicated with minimal hedging. A demon who performs false calm in a situation that warrants feeling is read as either deceptive or damaged.\n\nThis does not mean demons are chaotic or ungoverned. The expression is direct; the conduct is still subject to status logic. A low-ranked demon throwing a tantrum at a high-ranked noble is not expressing virtue — it's poor conduct, and the response will be proportionate. The distinction is between the emotion (legitimate, always) and the behavior (governed by rank, context, and consequence).\n\nDemon social calibration is faster and more legible than mortal social calibration because less is hidden. A demon meeting a potential ally reads their emotional display as data and adjusts accordingly. The dissonance that mortals experience navigating demon courts often comes from expecting subtlety and getting directness — a demon noble saying \"you've given me nothing worth wanting yet\" is not being rude; they're being efficient.\n\nRomantic expression follows the same logic. Affection is stated rather than implied. Courtship is conducted openly. Negotiation happens between beings who know what they want. The elaborate mortal performance of pretending not to want what you want is considered bizarre and slightly exhausting.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
